# Board artifacts must survive checkout byte-for-byte: BUILD_INFO.txt records
# their SHA-256, and an EOL-normalised .hwh would no longer match it.
*.bit -text
*.hwh -text
*.dcp -text
vivado/three_stage_combined/board_bundle/** -text

# Gate 4's fixtures, for the same reason: sw/GATE4_VECTORS.sha256 records
# their SHA-256 and board_gate_extract.py refuses to run on a mismatch, so an
# EOL-normalised manifest would fail the gate on Windows checkouts. The .txt
# files are covered too — they are vectors, not prose.
hls/integration/tb_bpe_tme_* -text
hls/template_match/tb_tme_*_hw.* -text

# Gate 5's fixtures, same reason: sw/GATE5_VECTORS.sha256 records their
# SHA-256 and board_gate_protocol.py refuses to run on a mismatch.
hls/integration/tb_proto_* -text

# Priority 4's pinned source snapshots, for the same reason again:
# run_hls_b1.tcl verifies each one with `sha256sum` over RAW bytes before it
# will compile it, and MANIFEST.sha256 records them too.  A CRLF checkout would
# change every digest and the A/B build would refuse to run on a fresh clone --
# which is the opposite of the reproducibility the snapshots exist for.
hls/template_match/b1_sources/*.cpp -text

# Priority 6's pinned tme_top snapshots, for exactly the same reason:
# run_hls_b0b.tcl verifies each with `sha256sum` over RAW bytes before it will
# compile it, and a CRLF checkout would change every digest.
hls/template_match/b0b_sources/*.cpp -text

# The Priority 0 evidence tools, for the same reason again: every trace
# directory's provenance.json records their SHA-256 under `code_sha256`, so a
# result can be tied to the code that produced it. core.autocrlf=true is set on
# this machine, which would hand a fresh checkout CRLF copies and break that
# binding on the first clone. Pin them byte-for-byte.
sw/tme_cycle_model.py -text
sw/tme_trace_capture.py -text

# Trace evidence, same reason: the run's provenance records the summary's
# SHA-256, so an EOL-normalised checkout would break the binding.
sw/trace_*_summary.csv -text
sw/trace_*_provenance.json -text

# Priority 4 (B1) evidence, all for the same reason as the rules above: the
# artifact IS its bytes, and logs/b1_20260818/MANIFEST.sha256 hashes these raw
# rather than EOL-normalised. A CRLF checkout would change every digest.
hls/template_match/tb_tme_*_phase_s.* -text
hls/template_match/template_match_b1/b1/impl/ip/*.zip -text

# Priority 5's packaged IP. The ZIP is binary; component.xml is TEXT that
# ships with LF endings, and logs/b2_20260819/MANIFEST.sha256 pins it at
# ea0d8a05...f9f771f4 -- a digest that is only reproducible from the bytes AMD
# emitted. A CRLF checkout would still verify (the manifest normalises .xml),
# but it would no longer be the packaged artifact, and the point of keeping it
# is that it IS the packaged artifact.
hls/template_match/template_match_b1_b2/b2/impl/ip/*.zip -text
hls/template_match/template_match_b1_b2/b2/impl/ip/component.xml -text

# Priority 6's packaged IP, for B2's reason exactly. This is the directory
# vivado_b0b_125.log names as the IP repository for the 8.000 ns run that did
# NOT close, so it is that route's input; logs/b0b_20260820/MANIFEST.sha256
# hashes the ZIP raw (c4df8ee4...a38f5e9) and would not verify from a CRLF
# checkout at all.
hls/template_match/template_match_b0b_b0b/b0b/impl/ip/*.zip -text
hls/template_match/template_match_b0b_b0b/b0b/impl/ip/component.xml -text

# The Vivado build script and the packaging script AS THEY RAN. Same argument
# as the board runner below: an immutable snapshot is only worth keeping if a
# fresh clone still holds the bytes that ran. build_tme_standalone.tcl.as_run
# is pinned at 1f2c9b47... by all three manifests, and the living generator has
# since moved -- that drift is why these snapshots exist.
logs/b1_20260818/build_tme_standalone.tcl.as_run -text
logs/b2_20260819/build_tme_standalone.tcl.as_run -text
logs/b0b_20260820/build_tme_standalone.tcl.as_run -text
logs/b0b_20260820/package_b0b.tcl.as_run -text

# The mutant-gate transcripts. RAW TOOL OUTPUT, bound by the B0b manifest and
# re-scored by `tme_b0b_mutants.py --replay`; the exit statuses beside them are
# half of every verdict.
hls/template_match/b0b_sources/mutants/csim_*.log -text
hls/template_match/b0b_sources/mutants/exit_codes.txt -text

# The board runner AS IT RAN. logs/b1_board_20260818/01_hashes_local.txt,
# 02_hashes_remote.txt and 03_run.txt all pin it at
# f7b00b0e7cdff74fc0940b46a5b01cb06b0f3fd61955c0ec41319340a8720153, taken with
# sha256sum over raw bytes on both the host and the board. The snapshot is only
# worth keeping if it still hashes to that on a fresh clone. The working copy
# is pinned too: it is what the next session will hash before loading.
logs/b1_board_20260818/tme_standalone_bringup.py.as_run -text
sw/tme_standalone_bringup.py -text

# Priority 4's retained transcripts.  These are RAW TOOL OUTPUT -- Vitis emits
# trailing whitespace and Vivado emits its own line endings, and `git diff
# --check` complains about both.  Do not clean them: MANIFEST.sha256 binds them,
# and a transcript edited for tidiness is no longer the thing that was produced.
# Pinned -text so a checkout reproduces the bytes rather than the tool's
# platform conventions being re-imposed by git.
logs/b1_20260818/** -text
logs/b1_board_20260818/** -text
logs/b1_rerun_20260818/** -text

# Priority 5 (B2), same rule and same reason: logs/b2_20260819/MANIFEST.sha256
# binds these, and the Vitis/Vivado transcripts in there are raw tool output
# whose trailing whitespace and line endings are part of what was produced.
logs/b2_20260819/** -text

# The B2 board session's transcripts, same rule: raw tool and shell output,
# hashed as-is on both the host and the board.
logs/b2_board_20260819/** -text

# The b2prod evidence sets, and one rule for all of them: an `.as_run`
# snapshot is a claim about BYTES.  `logs/b2prod_20260821/*/as_run/
# PAYLOAD.sha256` hashes the whole uploaded payload under the payload's own
# BARE names (`board_expect.py`), of which the `.as_run` files beside it are
# a renamed subset -- 5 of 10 for preflight, 9 of 32 for stage 1 -- so
# `sha256sum -c` in that directory does not run as-is and never did.  What
# does run is matching each `<name>.as_run` against the manifest line for
# `<name>`, and all 14 do.  That check is a byte comparison, so an
# EOL-normalised checkout breaks every one of them and turns the strongest
# thing these directories carry -- the code exactly as it ran, tied to the
# transcript by digest -- into something that cannot be verified.  Same
# rule, and the same reason, as the Priority 4/5/6 `.as_run` entries above.
# The rule is WHOLESALE over the three b2prod trees, not per file type, and
# that is the correction: picking out `*.as_run` and `*.txt` left
# `b2prod_20260822`'s 29 files and `b2prod_20260821/clock_probe`'s 11
# EOL-normalised while their siblings were not, which is an arbitrary split
# through one evidence set.  These directories are an ARCHIVE of what ran
# and what came back; nothing in them is edited after the fact, so there is
# no case where normalising helps and several where it destroys the only
# thing a file is good for.
logs/b2prod_20260821/** -text
logs/b2prod_20260822/** -text
logs/b2prod_20260823/** -text
logs/EVIDENCE_ALLOWLIST.txt -text
